

{"id":"9b11f451-4b20-45ed-9da0-6f240f823bf7","object":"chat.completion","created":1786438747,"model":"deepseek-v4-pro","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"record_id\": \"CA12_CORE_017\",\n  \"decisions\": [\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_01\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_03\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_04\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 17,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_02\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_02\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_09\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 22,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_03\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_01\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_02\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 11,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_04\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_03\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_05\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 7,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_05\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_03\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_07\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 10,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 0\n    },\n    {\n      \"mission_id\": \"MISSION_06\",\n      \"uav_id\": \"UAV_01\",\n      \"held\": false,\n      \"path_id\": \"PATH_01\",\n      \"depart_slot\": 6,\n      \"charge_blocks\": 1\n    }\n  ]\n}","reasoning_content":"We are given a task with fixed UAV assignments and order. The baseline provides a solution that maybe is infeasible due to intervention overrides (closures, capacity overrides, initial SOC overrides). We need to compute a repair: for each mission, either hold it (held=true, path_id null, depart_slot null, charge_blocks 0) or schedule it with a departure slot, path, and charge blocks (0 to max_charge_blocks=3). We must respect all constraints: energy (including incoming deadhead, service flight, reserve, post-service charging, battery capacity), corridor and pad capacities, charger capacities, corridor closures, release/due windows, turnaround time, sequence order (UAV missions in fixed order). Lexicographic optimization: minimize urgent holds, then total holds, then repair distance, then weighted tardiness, then delay plus charge wait, then energy. Since we are to return only one JSON, we need to find a feasible solution (or force holds if impossible). I will analyze each UAV's missions and constraints.\n\nUAV_01: assigned missions: MISSION_06 (seq 0, urgent, release 6, due 8), MISSION_03 (seq 1, routine, release 11, due 24). Base start: VP_06, available slot 6, initial SOC after override: 1.663115 kWh (was 2.75086 but overridden to 1.663115). Battery cap 16 kWh, reserve 1.5, empty energy 0.1 kWh/km, payload energy 0.026 kWh/km/kg, payload 0.5 kg both missions. Max wind:1. Path options for MISSION_06: PATH_01 (COR_02, distance 0.9 km, travel 2 slots, energy 0.112887, wind 1), PATH_03 (COR_09, 1.2 km, 2 slots, energy 0.150516, wind 1), PATH_08 (COR_08, 1.0 km, 3 slots, energy 0.113, wind 0). Note COR_08 is closed at slots 9-29, but MISSION_06 likely would depart slot >=6, so if departure slot 6-8? COR_08 closure starts at slot 9, but PATH_08 uses COR_08, which is closed at many slots. Also COR_02 is closed slots 8-29. COR_09 is closed slots 6-29? Check: COR_09 closed slots: 6,7,8,9,...,29. So COR_09 is closed from slot 6 onward. So MISSION_06 must avoid closed corridors for its travel slots. PATH_03 uses COR_09 (closed at slots 6-29) -> impossible if departure >=6 because travel slots 2 would occupy slots 6-7 if depart 6, etc. PATH_01 uses COR_02, which is closed at slots 8-29; could depart slot 6, travel 2 slots: slots 6-7, COR_02 closure starts slot 8, so okay? Check: COR_02 closure slots: 8,9,...29. So slots 6-7 are free. PATH_08 uses COR_08, closed from slot 9 onward; if depart slot 6, travel 3 slots: 6,7,8; COR_08 closed at 9-29, so slot 8 is open? Yes, closure starts slot 9. So both PATH_01 and PATH_08 might be possible, but PATH_01 duration 2 slots, PATH_08 duration 3 slots. Check due slot 8 for MISSION_06: finish travel by arrival slot = depart_slot + travel_slots. If PATH_01 depart 6 -> arrival slot 8 (depart 6, travel 2: occupies slots 6 and 7, then arrival at slot 8). Should be within due? presumably arrival must be <= due slot, maybe including turnaround? Normative SOC event order: incoming deadhead -> service flight -> reserve check -> destination turnaround -> post-service charge. So arrival after service flight is at origin? Actually, service flight is the mission's flight from origin to destination. After service flight, the UAV is at destination, then reserve check, then destination turnaround (which is turnaround time, I assume 1 slot?), then post-service charge. So the mission is considered \"completed\" after service flight? The due slot likely refers to the arrival at destination. Usually, due slot is the latest slot by which the mission must arrive at destination. So arrival slot = depart_slot + travel_slots must be <= due_slot. For MISSION_06 due=8. If depart 6 with PATH_01 (2 slots) arrival=8, ok. PATH_08 (3 slots) arrival=9, violates due=8. So PATH_08 is too slow. PATH_03 is also 2 slots, but closed. So only PATH_01 works. Also need to check pad capacity at VP_06 departure and VP_04 arrival. VP_06 pad capacity default 2, but overridden: pad_capacity_overrides has {\"capacity\":1,\"slot\":6,\"vertiport_id\":\"VP_06\"}. So at slot 6, VP_06 has only 1 pad capacity. But UAV_01 starts at VP_06 and has available slot 6. It needs to take off from VP_06 at slot 6. So it will occupy one pad at VP_06 at slot 6. Other UAVs might also need pad at VP_06. Check other UAVs: UAV_02 also starts at VP_06 available slot 6, UAV_03 starts at VP_06 available slot 6. All three start at VP_06 at slot 6? That's a problem because pad capacity at VP_06 is only 1 at slot 6. So simultaneous takeoffs from VP_06 at slot 6 are impossible. Baseline had different depart slots: MISSION_04 (UAV_03) depart 6, MISSION_06 (UAV_01) depart 6? No baseline: MISSION_06 depart 6, MISSION_04 depart 6? Actually baseline: MISSION_04 depart 6, MISSION_06 depart 6. Both depart from VP_06 at slot 6? MISSION_04 origin VP_06, MISSION_06 origin VP_06. So baseline had two takeoffs at slot 6 from VP_06 with pad capacity 1? That would be infeasible. So baseline might be infeasible, and we need to repair. So we must deconflict. Also UAV_02's MISSION_02 baseline depart 22 from VP_06, so no conflict at slot 6. So we need to shift one of UAV_01 or UAV_03 away from slot 6.\n\nUAV_01 mission sequence: MISSION_06 first, then MISSION_03. MISSION_06 due=8 very tight. If we delay MISSION_06 beyond depart 6, arrival >8, so it would be tardy, but due is 8. Is it allowed to be tardy? Weighted tardiness is penalized in objective, but lexicographically we first minimize urgent holds. MISSION_06 is urgent, so if we hold it, that's an urgent hold (bad). So we likely want to schedule it, even if tardy? The objective: \"Optimize the stated task lexicographically: urgent holds, total holds, repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy.\" So minimizing urgent holds is top priority. So we want to avoid holding urgent missions. If we cannot schedule on time, we schedule late, penalty is weighted tardiness. So we can depart MISSION_06 after release 6 as long as we can fly and reserve check ok. But due slot is just a target, not a hard constraint? Usually these problems treat due as a soft constraint, with tardiness penalty. Yes, the repair distance and weighted tardiness indicate tardiness is allowed but penalized. So we can depart after due slot, incurring tardiness.\n\nUAV_01: initial SOC 1.663115. Must have enough for incoming deadhead (none, since incoming_deadhead null), service flight energy, and after deducting service flight plus incoming deadhead, remaining must be >= reserve_kwh (1.5). Then we can charge after mission at destination. So for MISSION_06 with PATH_01: energy 0.112887. total required energy: incoming 0 + service 0.112887 = 0.112887. Initial SOC 1.663115 > 0.112887 + 1.5? 0.112887 + 1.5 = 1.612887, and 1.663115 > 1.612887, so sufficient. Reserve check passes. So it can fly. Then after arrival at VP_04, turnaround 1 slot, then post-service charge. So MISSION_06 can depart at 6, arrive slot 8, then turnaround slot 9? Actually travel slots 2: depart slot 6, flight occupies slots 6 and 7? Usually travel slots = duration, so if depart 6, arrival is 6 + travel_slots? In many formulations, arrival slot = depart_slot + travel_slots - 1? Let's check: In the baseline, MISSION_04 depart 6, path travel_slots 2, arrival? Need to infer. Also MISSION_06 depart 6, path travel_slots 2. Both from VP_06. If both depart at 6, slot usage: takeoff at slot 6, then travel corridor. Corridor capacity limited. But pad capacity 1 at VP_06 slot 6 so only one can take off. So we need to shift one. UAV_03 might have higher priority? No, order fixed but we can shift within release window. For MISSION_06 due=8, if we shift to slot 7, arrival=9 (with PATH_01 2 slots: depart 7, arrival 8? travel 2 slots: if depart 7, flights slots 7-8? arrival slot 9? I need to determine slot index convention. The horizon slots are 0-index? The available slot is 6 means slot index 6. Missions have release slot, due slot. Typically, departure slot is the slot when the UAV leaves the vertiport. The travel takes travel_slots slots, and arrival occurs at the end of the last travel slot. For example, if path travel_slots=2 and depart slot=6, then it occupies corridor slots 6 and 7, and arrives at the beginning of slot 8? Or arrives at slot 8? Many scheduling: if depart at slot t, and travel duration is d slots, then arrival at slot t+d. So MISSION_06 depart 6, travel 2 -> arrival 8. That matches due 8. So shift to depart 7: arrival 9, tardy 1. weighted tardiness penalized. So better to depart at 6 if possible. If pad capacity forced, UAV_01 might be the one to delay because UAV_03 might have urgent mission later? UAV_03 has MISSION_05 urgent due 12, MISSION_04 routine due 29, MISSION_01 routine due 26. So UAV_03 might also need depart early. So let's evaluate conflicts.\n\nWe need to assign depart slots considering pad capacity (VP_06 slot 6 cap 1). Only one UAV can take off from VP_06 at slot 6. So either UAV_01 (MISSION_06) or UAV_03 (MISSION_04) gets slot 6. The other must depart later, at slot 7 or later, provided release window (release slot 6 allows depart >=6). Both could potentially depart slot 7, but must check pad capacity at VP_06 slot 7: default 2, so two can depart at slot 7. So if both delay to 7, they'd conflict again if both need pad at 7. But pad capacity 2, so two is fine. So we could have one at 6, one at 7. Which one is more critical? MISSION_06 due 8, so delay makes it tardy; MISSION_04 due 29, so delay is okay. So it's better to give slot 6 to MISSION_06 (UAV_01). But we must also consider that after MISSION_06, UAV_01 will charge and then do MISSION_03. Its next mission MISSION_03 origin VP_04, release 11, due 24. So after MISSION_06, UAV_01 is at VP_04, turnaround, charge, then can depart for MISSION_03. The turnaround is 1 slot after arrival. So if MISSION_06 arrives at 8 (depart 6), turnaround occupies slot 9? Actually normative order: after service flight, reserve check, then destination turnaround, then post-service charge. Turnaround is 1 slot. So the UAV is unavailable during that turnaround slot. The charge blocks happen after turnaround. The departure for next mission can only happen after completion of post-service charge and any required travel to next origin. Since MISSION_03 origin is VP_04, same as MISSION_06 destination, so no deadhead needed (incoming deadhead is null anyway). So after MISSION_06 turnaround at slot 9, we can start charging at slot 10? Let's define: if arrival slot is A, then turnaround occupies slot A+1? Or is turnaround immediate? Typically, after arrival, the UAV goes into turnaround for the next slot and then is ready for next action? I'll interpret: Arrival slot is the slot when the UAV lands. Then turnaround takes 1 slot, meaning the UAV is unavailable during that slot. So it becomes available at slot arrival + 1? Actually, if it lands at slot 8, turnaround would be slot 8? Or slot 9? In some models, the turnaround occurs at the destination after the flight and before charging, and consumes one time slot. Since flight lands at slot 8, the UAV is on the pad during landing, then turnaround might happen in the next slot. The exact timing affects charging start. For safety, I'll assume: arrival at slot 8, then turnaround occupies slot 8 (or 9)? Usually, if travel slots = 2 and depart 6, slots 6-7 are travel, slot 8 is arrival and turnaround? The charge blocks can start after turnaround, so if turnaround takes 1 slot, charge can start at the slot after turnaround completion. I'll check baseline behavior: MISSION_04 (UAV_03) depart 6, path travel 2, so arrival 8. Then MISSION_05 (UAV_03) depart 9 from VP_04. That suggests turnaround and perhaps some charging? Let's see: MISSION_04 destination VP_04, MISSION_05 origin VP_04. Depart slot 9 for MISSION_05, so UAV_03 must be ready at VP_04 at slot 9 to depart. After MISSION_04 arrival at 8, turnaround 1 slot (maybe slot 8 or 9?), and then it can depart immediately without charging if energy allows. Baseline MISSION_05 charge_blocks=0. So UAV_03 didn't charge after MISSION_04. So if arrival at 8, it could turnaround in slot 8 and be ready by slot 9? That would mean turnaround occurs in the same slot as arrival? That seems quick. Alternatively, arrival at slot 8 means the UAV is on the ground at the beginning of slot 8, then turnaround takes slot 8, and it is available at slot 9. That works: depart 9. So I'll assume: after a flight of d slots departing slot t, the UAV arrives at destination at the beginning of slot t+d, then turnaround occupies that whole slot t+d, so the UAV becomes idle at slot t+d+1. So arrival slot conceptually is t+d, but turnaround consumes that slot. So the earliest next departure is t+d+1. In baseline: MISSION_04 depart 6, travel 2 -> arrives slot 8, turnaround slot 8, next depart slot 9 (MISSION_05). That matches. Also MISSION_06 depart 6, travel 2 -> arrive slot 8, turnaround slot 8, next depart for MISSION_03 could be slot 9 or later. But MISSION_03 release is 11, so no rush. So UAV_01 after MISSION_06 depart 6, turnaround 8, available at 9, can charge starting slot 9. But MISSION_03 origin is VP_04, same, so no travel. So it can depart at 11 or later. We'll need to ensure SOC after charging meets requirements.\n\nNow UAV_01 initial SOC 1.663115, MISSION_06 path energy 0.112887. After service, remaining = 1.550228, minus reserve 1.5 leaves 0.050228. That is minimal. Then after turnaround, charging. At VP_04, charger capacity 2 (default, no overrides). So we can charge. Each charge block adds 1.2 kWh. max 3 blocks. To do MISSION_03 (energy? MISSION_03 path PATH_02 (only one, distance 0.8, energy 0.100344) plus reserve 1.5. So total needed energy = 1.600344. At start of MISSION_03, SOC must be >= 0.100344 + 1.5 = 1.600344. Current SOC after MISSION_06 service is 1.550228, so need at least 0.050116 kWh more. One charge block gives 1.2, more than enough. But battery capacity is 16 kWh, so far less than capacity, so fine. So we can charge 1 block after MISSION_06 to be safe. But could we do without charging? 1.550228 < 1.600344, so need at least some charge. So charge_blocks for MISSION_06 must be >=1. Actually charge_blocks are attached to the mission at its destination after turnaround. So for MISSION_06, we decide charge_blocks (0-3). If charge_blocks=1, then after turnaround (slot 8), the UAV charges for 1 block, which presumably takes some time? The charging time per block? The problem doesn't specify charge time, just charge amount per block (kWh) and charger capacity. Usually, each charge block consumes one slot? In previous similar tasks, charge_blocks duration is assumed to be equal to the number of blocks, and each block takes one slot. Yes, typical: a charge block represents charging for one time slot and provides a certain kWh. So if we set charge_blocks=1, that means after turnaround, the UAV will charge for 1 slot, occupying a charger at VP_04 for that slot, and then be ready for next mission. Since MISSION_03 release is 11, we have time. So we need to allocate a charger slot at VP_04 for that charge block. Charger capacity is 2. Check other UAVs might also need VP_04 charger. We'll manage. So after MISSION_06, if arrival 8, turnaround slot 8, then charge block 1 would occupy slot 9? But then UAV would be free at slot 10, which is before release 11. So it could depart at 11. That's fine.\n\nNow, UAV_01 initial SOC override is 1.663115. That's lower than baseline's 2.75086. In baseline, MISSION_06 depart 6 with PATH_03 (which used COR_09) but COR_09 is now closed slots 6-29. So baseline path is infeasible due to closure. Also initial SOC is lower, but still enough for PATH_01. So we'll likely use PATH_01.\n\nNow UAV_03: missions in order: MISSION_04 (seq 0, routine, release 6, due 29), MISSION_05 (seq 1, urgent, release 9, due 12), MISSION_01 (seq 2, routine, release 17, due 26). Base VP_06. Initial SOC: 10.66308 kWh. Battery cap 22 kWh, reserve 2.5 kWh, charge_kwh_per_block 0.95. UAV_03 has different parameters: empty_energy 0.145, payload_energy 0.012, payload 0.5 kg both. Max wind 2. Path options for MISSION_04: PATH_05 (COR_07, 1.08 km, travel 2 slots, energy 0.16308, wind 0), max_wind 0, so compatible. PATH_05 has energy 0.16308. That's it. Check corridors: COR_07 is not listed in closed corridors. So it's open. So MISSION_04 can use PATH_05. Also should check if COR_07 capacity default 2, fine. So MISSION_04 depart slot? Need to deconflict with UAV_01 at VP_06 slot 6. If UAV_01 takes slot 6, UAV_03 can take slot 7 (since pad cap 2 at slot 7). Release is 6, so depart 7 allowed. Travel 2 slots: depart 7, arrivals slot 9 (7+2). Then turnaround slot 9, available after 10. Next mission MISSION_05 origin VP_04 (same as MISSION_04 destination). MISSION_05 release 9, due 12. So if we depart MISSION_04 at 7, arrival 9, turnaround 9, next available 10. That is after release 9, so can depart for MISSION_05 at 10. But MISSION_05 path options: PATH_06 (COR_01, 4 slots, energy 0.32163, wind 1), PATH_07 (COR_10, 1 slot, energy 0.160815), PATH_10 (COR_06, 2 slots, energy 0.160815). COR_06 closed slots 9-32 (many), COR_10 open? Not listed as closed, so open. COR_01 open? Not closed. So PATH_07 (1 slot) or PATH_06 (4 slots) or PATH_10 (2 slots but closed). PATH_10 uses COR_06, which is closed from slot 9 onward; if depart slot 9 or later, it would be closed. So PATH_10 is infeasible if depart >=9. PATH_07: travel 1 slot, COR_10. That is quick, arrival slot = depart +1. Due 12, so if depart 10, arrival 11, ok. Energy 0.160815. Total energy needed: 0.160815 + reserve 2.5 = 2.660815. After MISSION_04, UAV_03 SOC: initial 10.66308 - MISSION_04 energy (0.16308) = 10.500. Reserve check after MISSION_04: 10.500 - 2.5 = 8.0 >0, ok. Then after turnaround at slot 9, we have no charging between MISSION_04 and MISSION_05 if we depart at 10. Charge is post-service after MISSION_04 at destination VP_04, but if we want to charge, that would delay MISSION_05 departure. We could choose charge_blocks for MISSION_04, but then MISSION_05 would have to depart later. MISSION_05 is urgent, due 12, so we want to depart as soon as possible to avoid tardiness. So likely no charge after MISSION_04. SOC after MISSION_04 is 10.5, well above 2.66, so no need to charge. So we can set charge_blocks=0 for MISSION_04. Then MISSION_05 departure at slot 10 (arrive 11). That is tardy by 0 since due 12? Actually due 12, arrival 11 is on time. Weighted tardiness 0. So that works. But what about MISSION_05 path? PATH_07 uses COR_10, travel 1 slot at wind 1, energy 0.160815. Check wind energy multiplier: UAV_03 wind_energy_multiplier 0.065. Path energy already given includes wind? The path's \"energy_kwh\" is computed based on corridor wind and distance, I assume it's the total service flight energy including wind. So we use the given energy_kwh. So fine.\n\nNow after MISSION_05, UAV_03 is at VP_01 (destination of MISSION_05). Then turnaround at VP_01 after arrival? Arrival slot 11 (depart 10, travel 1 -> arrive 11? Actually depart 10, travel 1 slot, so it occupies slot 10, lands at beginning of 11? With turnaround taking slot 11, available at slot 12. Next mission MISSION_01 origin VP_01, release 17, due 26. So there is time to charge. MISSION_05 charge_blocks we might set to something to prepare for MISSION_01. MISSION_01 path: PATH_04 (COR_05, 3.15 km, travel 1 slot, energy 0.506567, wind 1). Total energy needed for MISSION_01: 0.506567 + reserve 2.5 = 3.006567. After MISSION_05, SOC: after MISSION_04 SOC was 10.5, then MISSION_05 energy 0.160815, so remaining = 10.5 - 0.160815 = 10.339185. So plenty. No charging needed. So charge_blocks for MISSION_05 could be 0. Then UAV_03 can depart MISSION_01 at release 17. But it will idle from slot 12 to 17. So fine.\n\nBut we need to consider pad capacity at VP_06 for UAV_03 departure at slot 7. VP_06 pad capacity at slot 7 is default 2. UAV_02 may also depart later. Check.\n\nNow UAV_02: missions: MISSION_02 (seq 0, routine, release 22, due 29). Base VP_06, initial SOC 7.5 kWh, battery 16, reserve 1.5, charge 1.2, empty 0.1, payload 0.026, payload 0.5. Path: PATH_09 (COR_04, distance 1.0, travel 2 slots, energy 0.12543, wind 1). COR_04 open? Not in closures. So ok. Release 22, so depart >=22. Origin VP_06. Requires enough SOC: 0.12543 + 1.5 = 1.62543, initial 7.5 >, so fine. No charging needed. So we can schedule at release 22. Pad capacity at VP_06 slot 22 default 2, so okay. No conflicts.\n\nNow we must ensure sequence order: each UAV's missions must be in the order given. For 